A||||19||TOM||will 88 brakes fit?||||1||
Z||000000||TOM||01-18-2000||09:49 PM||||Can you swap the calipers and rotors from an 88 to an earlier one.||||reg||1
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||01-18-2000||10:53 PM||||Interesting. The 88 rotors are thicker (twice as thick?) than the other rotors. Would that be a concern?||||reg||1
Z||000002||lawrence||01-18-2000||11:02 PM||||Sorry, Tom, but they won't fit.  First, the calipers have a different bolt pattern, so they won't even go on without a special mounting bracket.  Second, the rotors, aside from being vented, are slipped onto the hub like the rears on the earlier cars.  You would not be able to put them onto the fronts without machining the old rotor off.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<p>[This message has been edited by lawrence (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||01-18-2000||11:19 PM||||The mounting bracket adapter is no problem. Why cant you use the hub from the 88. To machine the rotor off and make the hub the right size is no problem. Is that it?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phaeton||01-19-2000||04:44 AM||||Machine the rotor off, cut down the sides so the new rotor will slide over, and then machine the face back far enough to allow the new rotor to seat in the exact same spot.  There has to be a better way to get bigger brakes than removing metal from a forging even though I'm sure it was built stronger than it had to be.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||01-19-2000||02:47 PM||||I have always wondered what exactly people mean by "vented" brakes on the '88s. Are you talking about the space between the rotor faces? Thats all I can think of, and if so, are you saying the '84-'87 brake rotors are just one solid piece? I've always figured vented rotors were standard on all post '70s cars. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||01-19-2000||02:52 PM||||Yes and Yes. 88s are the "typical" rotor design with openings between the 2 rotor faces. 84-87 are one solid piece. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||WGraffiKs||01-19-2000||04:45 PM||||So will the GrandAM brake swap (I assume it works on the front only) fit the 88, the 84-87, or ALL of the above?<BR>If I try it on the front of my 86, will I need to dink with the proportioning valve?||||reg||5
Z||000008||batboy||01-19-2000||05:42 PM||||The Grand Am brake swap is for the 84-87 Fieros, because the 88's already have the improved brakes. Actually the rear brakes are easier than the front (a bolt-in except for a little grinding on the caliper mounting bracket), but you lose the use of your e-brake.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||01-19-2000||05:44 PM||||Oh yeah! If you only upgrade the front brakes, then yes, you have to use an adjustable proportioning valve.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||01-19-2000||06:21 PM||||If you're going to machine adaptors and rotors,why would you want to settle for '88 brakes?<BR>Subaru Legacy rotors will fit over a machined(5" OD)Fiero hub with longer studs,and  ATE,BMW/Volvo,4 piston calipers can be adapted.A Blazer diesel master cylinder will give the same ratio as the stock arrangement.A proportioning valve may be needed for the rear.<BR>I have all the parts and will let you know how it works out after I swap in some different springs to help get rid of some of the dive in front.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||01-20-2000||01:39 AM||||because the Grand Am swap is cheap(costs much less than the 88 brake setup)!  best bang for the buck.||||reg||1
Z||000012||WGraffiKs||01-20-2000||03:45 PM||||What is a good source for the adjustable proportioning valve?  <BR>I assume it bolts right up with no alterations needed?<BR>Anyone here done it?<BR>I'll look back at the past posts for detailed info, but what year(s) Grand Am will fit?||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||01-20-2000||05:29 PM||||JEGS has adjustable p-valves. Late 80's Grand Am and Berettas will work (I'm thinking 86-90). I asked for calipers and rotors for a  1988 Grand Am and all my parts fit ok. Go to my website to the favorite Fiero links page. There is a link there that details the whole brake swap.||||reg||1
Z||000014||lowCG||01-20-2000||07:01 PM||||So far I've got a total of oh,less than $400 into the swap;<BR>new master cylinder,rotors,teflon/stainless braided lines,used calipers rebuilt.Not bad for four piston,11" vented rotor,stopping power with nearly twice the piston area(2 5/8" equivalent piston diameter)of the FWD car's components,which,after all,are only slightly larger than the original Fiero stuff.If I need more,I can always find a set of carbon metallic pads to improve the stopping power.<BR>The stock brakes work allright around town,but any performance use with a good set of tires and a well sorted suspension requires a bit more than any factory/production GM brake parts can handle.<BR>My buck stops here!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||TOM||01-20-2000||08:06 PM||||$400 I could probably do the whol thing for around $100 with the 88 parts. I dont think I spen $400 on the turbo configuration||||reg||1
Z||000016||lawrence||01-20-2000||10:36 PM||||I'm not that impressed with the CarboMet pads.  They are quiet and they don't dust very much, but they need to get hot before they work very well.  Unfortulately, they have about 90% life left on them or I would pitch them and go with stock pads.<P>-L||||reg||1
Z||000017||lowCG||01-21-2000||05:22 PM||||When I was 18,I test drove this Alfa sedan that had a set of Repco Metalmasters on it,and the owner who raced it,didn't tell me.I sailed through the first intersection as they were cold yet.Changed out those pads right away.I understand they have a grade that will still work when cold to remedy this sort of thing;Hawk Brakes,I believe it is.I probably won't be needing them with the bigger brakes though.||||reg||1
Z||000018||lawrence||01-21-2000||06:58 PM||||Hawk Head Brakes?  That's the company that makes the CarboMets.  Maybe they have a 'cold' set of pads for our cars.<P>-L||||reg||1
Z||000019||lowCG||01-21-2000||09:00 PM||||It's either that  brand or Performance Friction,but am pretty sure it's the Hawk.<BR>The compound was developed for rallye and driving school use where easy modulation was more critical.Seem to recall the compounds being listed in color codes for the different heat ratings,blue maybe.<BR>||||reg||1
